1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,200
Civilians under attack.

2
00:00:03,201 --> 00:00:06,300
The images are surreal and unsettling.

3
00:00:06,301 --> 00:00:09,200
In the 1950s, the threat of a Soviet nuclear attack

4
00:00:09,201 --> 00:00:11,440
spurred an arms race, and Americans

5
00:00:11,441 --> 00:00:13,340
learned to duck and cover.

6
00:00:13,341 --> 00:00:16,440
Today, there are new enemies wielding new weapons.

7
00:00:16,441 --> 00:00:18,960
Duck and cover has been replaced by duct tape

8
00:00:18,961 --> 00:00:20,160
and plastic sheeting.

9
00:00:20,161 --> 00:00:22,760
And there's a new technological arms race underway,

10
00:00:22,761 --> 00:00:26,120
as science tries to stay one step ahead of the terrorist

11
00:00:26,121 --> 00:00:27,160
threat.

12
00:00:27,161 --> 00:00:29,760
Find out what technology can do to keep you and your family

13
00:00:29,761 --> 00:00:30,660
safe.

14
00:00:47,680 --> 00:00:51,740
June 22, 2001.

15
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:55,020
A senior level war game codenamed Dark Winter

16
00:00:55,120 --> 00:00:59,760
gets underway at Andrews Air Force Base in Washington, DC.

17
00:00:59,761 --> 00:01:03,060
Wargaming is a time-honored practice in the US military

18
00:01:03,160 --> 00:01:06,200
to test readiness and strategy.

19
00:01:06,300 --> 00:01:08,000
But this scenario is different.

20
00:01:08,100 --> 00:01:12,200
This time, the fictional enemy is a terrorist group.

21
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:15,100
The target is the American public.

22
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:20,620
And the weapon is a biological agent, smallpox.

23
00:01:20,720 --> 00:01:23,720
The fictional scenario begins with terrorists unleashing

24
00:01:23,820 --> 00:01:26,720
the deadly virus in shopping malls in three states.

25
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:31,760
The purpose of the dark winter exercise

26
00:01:31,860 --> 00:01:34,160
was to see how ready the country was to deal

27
00:01:34,260 --> 00:01:35,860
with this new type of threat.

28
00:01:35,960 --> 00:01:38,160
How ready were we?

29
00:01:38,260 --> 00:01:39,160
We shall see.

30
00:01:52,860 --> 00:01:55,360
The threat of large-scale attacks on American civilians

31
00:01:55,460 --> 00:01:57,560
is not new.

32
00:01:57,561 --> 00:02:00,200
Long before the rise of international terrorist groups,

33
00:02:00,300 --> 00:02:02,800
Americans had plenty to fear from foreign armies.

34
00:02:06,600 --> 00:02:08,540
And many of the technologies being developed

35
00:02:08,640 --> 00:02:12,040
to protect Americans today are based on concepts that date

36
00:02:12,140 --> 00:02:13,740
back to the World Wars.

37
00:02:13,840 --> 00:02:16,920
A soldier from World War I would easily recognize

38
00:02:17,020 --> 00:02:18,420
the gas masks of today.

39
00:02:21,820 --> 00:02:25,080
And the recent creation of a Department of Homeland Security

40
00:02:25,081 --> 00:02:29,080
wouldn't have surprised President Franklin Roosevelt.

41
00:02:29,180 --> 00:02:31,380
FDR called his homeland security outfit

42
00:02:31,480 --> 00:02:34,720
the Office of Civilian Defense.

43
00:02:34,820 --> 00:02:38,620
It was formed in May of 1941, even before America's entry

44
00:02:38,720 --> 00:02:41,700
into World War II, to protect against sneak attacks

45
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:44,060
on American cities by the Axis powers.

46
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:50,300
During the Cold War, American civilians

47
00:02:50,400 --> 00:02:54,080
had a new reason to be fearful.

48
00:02:54,081 --> 00:02:56,820
They had witnessed the destructive force of the atomic bombs

49
00:02:56,920 --> 00:02:59,860
dropped on Japanese cities to end World War II,

50
00:02:59,960 --> 00:03:01,620
and they knew the Soviet Union now

51
00:03:01,720 --> 00:03:07,020
had the ability to devastate American cities in the same way.

52
00:03:07,120 --> 00:03:09,600
Technology couldn't stop a nuclear bomb,

53
00:03:09,700 --> 00:03:11,560
but it could give residents early warning

54
00:03:11,660 --> 00:03:13,360
of an impending air raid.

55
00:03:13,460 --> 00:03:17,040
A national radar system was developed.

56
00:03:17,140 --> 00:03:19,180
A radiological service was formed,

57
00:03:19,280 --> 00:03:24,080
using early radiation detectors to test for nuclear fallout.

58
00:03:24,081 --> 00:03:26,540
An especially trained chemical corps prepared

59
00:03:26,640 --> 00:03:28,020
against a possible gas attack.

60
00:03:31,320 --> 00:03:32,980
The best the technology had to offer

61
00:03:33,080 --> 00:03:36,820
for surviving a nuclear blast was concrete, and lots of it.

62
00:03:39,820 --> 00:03:42,640
In Los Angeles, the local Civil Defense Planning Board

63
00:03:42,740 --> 00:03:45,140
drafted a survival plan to prepare residents

64
00:03:45,240 --> 00:03:48,640
for nuclear attack.

65
00:03:48,740 --> 00:03:51,280
They recommended civilians build underground shelters

66
00:03:51,281 --> 00:03:55,880
of reinforced concrete covered with three feet of earth

67
00:03:55,980 --> 00:03:58,980
and supplied with air intake filters.

68
00:03:59,080 --> 00:04:04,320
A two-person unit was estimated to cost $6,000.

69
00:04:04,420 --> 00:04:06,460
As recently as 1980, the government

70
00:04:06,560 --> 00:04:08,360
was still recommending concrete shelters

71
00:04:08,460 --> 00:04:11,840
for protection from nuclear attack.

72
00:04:11,940 --> 00:04:16,180
They issued guides to homeowners on how to build them.

73
00:04:16,280 --> 00:04:18,780
There's really no technology at all.

74
00:04:18,880 --> 00:04:19,980
It's basic.

75
00:04:19,981 --> 00:04:22,380
If you want to sit down, there's a wood bench.

76
00:04:22,480 --> 00:04:24,780
So you want to lay down, there's a cot.

77
00:04:24,880 --> 00:04:27,720
If you want some water, there's a bucket over the corner

78
00:04:27,820 --> 00:04:28,880
with water in it.

79
00:04:28,980 --> 00:04:33,420
So that's the technology they had at that time.

80
00:04:33,520 --> 00:04:36,220
When the Cold War thawed, bomb shelters were abandoned,

81
00:04:36,320 --> 00:04:39,740
and many expected a time of extended peace.

82
00:04:39,840 --> 00:04:42,540
But civilians here and abroad are facing new threats,

83
00:04:42,640 --> 00:04:45,240
not just from governments, but from terrorist groups.

84
00:04:45,241 --> 00:04:49,840
Civilians today are warned to be prepared for a chemical,

85
00:04:49,940 --> 00:04:53,240
biological, or radiological attack.

86
00:04:53,340 --> 00:04:55,820
And they're looking to technology for new ways

87
00:04:55,920 --> 00:04:58,580
to protect their homes and families.

88
00:04:58,680 --> 00:05:00,480
In the face of these new threats,

89
00:05:00,580 --> 00:05:02,620
some wealthy individuals in Los Angeles

90
00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:08,100
are taking the old bomb shelter concept to the extreme.

91
00:05:08,200 --> 00:05:10,400
We're building a terrorist-proof home here,

92
00:05:10,500 --> 00:05:12,860
and I'd like to point out some of the items

93
00:05:12,861 --> 00:05:15,960
some of the items that we're providing this home

94
00:05:15,961 --> 00:05:18,000
to make it terrorist-proof.

95
00:05:18,001 --> 00:05:20,340
For years, Bill Rigdon and his company,

96
00:05:20,341 --> 00:05:23,040
Building Consensus, designed panic rooms

97
00:05:23,041 --> 00:05:25,400
to shield celebrities and other wealthy clients

98
00:05:25,401 --> 00:05:27,500
from home invaders.

99
00:05:27,501 --> 00:05:31,740
Lately, his clients have been calling with new fears.

100
00:05:31,741 --> 00:05:34,340
Someone had mentioned on the media

101
00:05:34,341 --> 00:05:37,080
that we were going to be attacked with a nuclear bomb

102
00:05:37,081 --> 00:05:38,120
here in LA.

103
00:05:38,121 --> 00:05:39,780
So we got a call.

104
00:05:39,781 --> 00:05:46,740
the next day a guy was concerned that he was going to be attacked by a nuclear bomb,

105
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:50,871
that he personally felt that he was going to be attacked.

106
00:05:50,872 --> 00:05:53,520
We went to that residence and basically

107
00:05:53,521 --> 00:06:00,820
made that room nuclear proof. Former FBI agent Mark Llewellyn works with Rigdon.

108
00:06:01,640 --> 00:06:07,340
In today's environment, with all the terrorist alerts, a lot of people have come to us looking

109
00:06:07,341 --> 00:06:12,020
for advice and consultation on how they can better be safe in their homes, how they can

110
00:06:12,021 --> 00:06:13,840
protect their families.

111
00:06:13,841 --> 00:06:18,900
Some of the advice we give them is very common sense type advice, just like you hear it on

112
00:06:18,901 --> 00:06:23,300
the news every day, to be vigilant, to look out for strange things around you.

113
00:06:23,301 --> 00:06:28,440
And then when we get to the far extreme, there's the safe room, and that's another mechanism

114
00:06:28,441 --> 00:06:32,540
that they can add in their repertoire of things to do to protect their families.

115
00:06:32,541 --> 00:06:35,080
Now we're going to be over here looking at the side of the home.

116
00:06:35,081 --> 00:06:37,480
to give us a rare look inside a safe room

117
00:06:37,580 --> 00:06:39,560
that's in the early stages of construction

118
00:06:39,660 --> 00:06:42,560
outside of Los Angeles.

119
00:06:42,660 --> 00:06:45,460
You can see by walking down this corridor how wide it is.

120
00:06:45,560 --> 00:06:47,460
And that's for a reason, because we

121
00:06:47,560 --> 00:06:52,460
want to have this access way into this part of the house

122
00:06:52,560 --> 00:06:55,060
be very usable during an emergency.

123
00:06:55,160 --> 00:06:56,700
If it's late at night, you're groggy,

124
00:06:56,800 --> 00:06:58,500
you're walking around, you're trying to figure out

125
00:06:58,600 --> 00:07:02,580
and orientate yourself, this is where the panic room door

126
00:07:02,680 --> 00:07:03,640
will be.

127
00:07:03,641 --> 00:07:04,640
It's in a pocket.

128
00:07:04,740 --> 00:07:07,080
It's going to slam out and close.

129
00:07:07,180 --> 00:07:08,480
You better not be standing here.

130
00:07:08,580 --> 00:07:11,040
And it is blast proof.

131
00:07:11,140 --> 00:07:12,720
It's bullet proof.

132
00:07:12,820 --> 00:07:13,980
It's slam proof.

133
00:07:14,080 --> 00:07:17,120
It's everything.

134
00:07:17,220 --> 00:07:19,420
A popular item among Rigdon's clients

135
00:07:19,520 --> 00:07:21,700
are anti-ballistic wall panels.

136
00:07:21,800 --> 00:07:25,100
They're made of Kevlar, the same material as bulletproof vests

137
00:07:25,200 --> 00:07:28,600
and are blast resistant and fireproof.

138
00:07:28,700 --> 00:07:31,040
Here we have the material that we're going to bolt

139
00:07:31,140 --> 00:07:32,100
onto the walls here.

140
00:07:32,101 --> 00:07:33,640
We took this down to the firing range

141
00:07:33,641 --> 00:07:36,500
and fired a .22 caliber bullet here.

142
00:07:36,501 --> 00:07:45,540
We fired a .357, .44, and also another .44 round and a .45.

143
00:07:45,541 --> 00:07:48,680
You can see that also you have different thicknesses

144
00:07:48,681 --> 00:07:49,980
of this material.

145
00:07:49,981 --> 00:07:52,820
This is a very thin piece, and this is a level one.

146
00:07:52,821 --> 00:07:55,520
This will stop like a .22 round.

147
00:07:55,521 --> 00:07:58,300
This material is level two.

148
00:07:58,301 --> 00:08:03,040
It'll stop up to a larger caliber handgun.

149
00:08:03,140 --> 00:08:04,500
And then we go to level three.

150
00:08:04,600 --> 00:08:06,000
You can see the thickness here.

151
00:08:06,100 --> 00:08:08,940
It's about double of what we have here.

152
00:08:09,040 --> 00:08:11,880
The reason we're going thicker on this particular project,

153
00:08:11,980 --> 00:08:13,040
they want that.

154
00:08:13,140 --> 00:08:17,980
They want that additional protection and the fireproofing.

155
00:08:18,080 --> 00:08:20,560
In addition to absorbing a conventional bomb blast,

156
00:08:20,660 --> 00:08:22,560
some clients want added protection

157
00:08:22,660 --> 00:08:27,020
from chemical, biological, or radiological attack.

158
00:08:27,021 --> 00:08:31,060
We're going to come into this room if there is a germ warfare

159
00:08:31,160 --> 00:08:34,520
attack down in LA, or if there is a dirty bomb,

160
00:08:34,620 --> 00:08:38,140
or an explosion of an atomic bomb, a suitcase bomb down

161
00:08:38,240 --> 00:08:39,140
in LA.

162
00:08:39,240 --> 00:08:42,400
You can come into this room, which is going to have a thick

163
00:08:42,500 --> 00:08:43,900
plastic material.

164
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:45,740
It's going to come up from the floor.

165
00:08:45,840 --> 00:08:47,340
It's going to come up to the walls.

166
00:08:47,440 --> 00:08:50,840
And what that does, it seals off anything coming inside,

167
00:08:50,940 --> 00:08:52,340
any germ warfare.

168
00:08:52,440 --> 00:08:56,820
Layer two will be the lead lining that's going to go over

169
00:08:56,821 --> 00:08:59,320
that plastic to protect you from radiation.

170
00:08:59,420 --> 00:09:01,860
Layer three will be the Kevlar.

171
00:09:01,960 --> 00:09:04,920
Those bullet-resistant, blast-resistant panels,

172
00:09:05,020 --> 00:09:07,160
and layer four will be the drywall.

173
00:09:07,260 --> 00:09:10,160
That will go over the Kevlar panels,

174
00:09:10,260 --> 00:09:13,740
and you will have a totally secured room.

175
00:09:13,840 --> 00:09:16,080
And thanks to advances in anti-ballistic glass

176
00:09:16,180 --> 00:09:21,380
technology, you can now get a safe room with a view.

177
00:09:21,480 --> 00:09:23,880
We're going to be installing these windows.

178
00:09:23,980 --> 00:09:26,380
And the reason I'm not going to hold up the window

179
00:09:26,381 --> 00:09:28,620
The window in this opening is the density.

180
00:09:28,720 --> 00:09:30,320
They're so heavy.

181
00:09:30,420 --> 00:09:33,120
This window is absorbing a blast generated by a bomb

182
00:09:33,220 --> 00:09:36,320
nearly 1 1⁄2 times as powerful as the one that destroyed

183
00:09:36,420 --> 00:09:38,820
the Oklahoma City Federal Building.

184
00:09:38,920 --> 00:09:42,620
I'm going to set this down before my arm falls off.

185
00:09:42,720 --> 00:09:44,340
After an attack, a family might have

186
00:09:44,440 --> 00:09:47,440
to spend as long as 72 hours in their safe room.

187
00:09:47,540 --> 00:09:52,140
Clean air and water are essential.

188
00:09:52,240 --> 00:09:54,780
Rigdon offers high-efficiency particulate air,

189
00:09:54,781 --> 00:09:58,180
or HEPA filters to remove chemicals and bacteria

190
00:09:58,181 --> 00:10:00,120
from the air.

191
00:10:00,121 --> 00:10:04,020
Anthrax, germ warfare, whatever they're throwing out there,

192
00:10:04,021 --> 00:10:05,700
we're going to be protected in here.

193
00:10:05,701 --> 00:10:07,720
What if someone poisons the water?

194
00:10:07,721 --> 00:10:09,520
If you look at the wall here, these pipes

195
00:10:09,521 --> 00:10:11,400
are going through a filtration system.

196
00:10:11,401 --> 00:10:13,700
It's going to take all the contaminants out

197
00:10:13,701 --> 00:10:17,560
and put in a separate tank so you have a long duration

198
00:10:17,561 --> 00:10:21,280
of water you'll be able to use in your home in case someone

199
00:10:21,281 --> 00:10:24,780
contaminates the water supply that's coming in here.

200
00:10:24,781 --> 00:10:27,420
Terrorist-proof safe rooms are expensive.

201
00:10:27,520 --> 00:10:30,720
This one costs over $500,000.

202
00:10:30,820 --> 00:10:32,480
For high-profile people whose homes

203
00:10:32,580 --> 00:10:34,920
might be direct targets of terrorist attack,

204
00:10:35,020 --> 00:10:37,020
the expense may be justified.

205
00:10:37,120 --> 00:10:40,020
But what about for the average citizen?

206
00:10:40,120 --> 00:10:41,940
If you're a movie star or something like that,

207
00:10:42,040 --> 00:10:44,440
yeah, you could, in fact, be more likely to be targeted

208
00:10:44,540 --> 00:10:48,040
as someone who might use conventional or unconventional

209
00:10:48,140 --> 00:10:49,740
means to attack you.

210
00:10:49,840 --> 00:10:51,440
I think for the general population, though,

211
00:10:51,540 --> 00:10:53,880
to think about having a safe room, again,

212
00:10:53,881 --> 00:10:56,580
again is probably overkill.

213
00:10:56,581 --> 00:10:59,580
You have to think of where is the most logical place

214
00:10:59,581 --> 00:11:01,280
for a terrorist attack.

215
00:11:01,281 --> 00:11:04,020
And certainly it's not going to be out in the countryside

216
00:11:04,021 --> 00:11:06,120
on somebody's lone farm.

217
00:11:06,121 --> 00:11:09,420
It's more likely to happen in a major metropolitan area.

218
00:11:09,421 --> 00:11:11,340
It may be an entertainment area.

219
00:11:11,341 --> 00:11:13,840
It may be a place that has amusement park close to it.

220
00:11:13,841 --> 00:11:16,340
However, terrorists may target an area

221
00:11:16,341 --> 00:11:18,040
which is close enough to the home

222
00:11:18,041 --> 00:11:19,440
where they may feel threatened

223
00:11:19,441 --> 00:11:21,140
and they may feel they need this safe room

224
00:11:21,141 --> 00:11:22,440
to protect their family.

225
00:11:22,441 --> 00:11:24,400
We're looking out here to Los Angeles right now.

226
00:11:24,401 --> 00:11:27,140
If there was a terrorist act somewhere in Los Angeles,

227
00:11:27,141 --> 00:11:28,500
you could see it from right here.

228
00:11:28,501 --> 00:11:31,180
But you'd be safe in your home that you knew no one

229
00:11:31,181 --> 00:11:31,881
could come and get you.

230
00:11:34,880 --> 00:11:38,080
The engineers at BMW have taken the safe room concept

231
00:11:38,081 --> 00:11:38,800
out on the road.

232
00:11:41,540 --> 00:11:44,000
The 760i high security model seems

233
00:11:44,001 --> 00:11:46,100
to have rolled out of a science fiction movie.

234
00:11:52,440 --> 00:11:56,580
It can repel bullets and grenades.

235
00:11:56,680 --> 00:12:00,580
And high-tech sensors constantly sniff the air for a gas attack,

236
00:12:00,680 --> 00:12:03,980
hermetically sealing the cabin at the first sign of danger

237
00:12:04,080 --> 00:12:09,160
and providing passengers with fresh, breathable oxygen.

238
00:12:09,260 --> 00:12:11,160
Not yet available in the United States,

239
00:12:11,260 --> 00:12:14,620
this fortress on wheels is favored by heads of state

240
00:12:14,720 --> 00:12:16,860
and costs over $200,000.

241
00:12:16,861 --> 00:12:21,860
$500,000.

242
00:12:21,960 --> 00:12:24,260
Next, a look at affordable technologies

243
00:12:24,360 --> 00:12:25,960
to safeguard your home and family.

244
00:12:32,700 --> 00:12:35,240
It's day two of the dark winter war game,

245
00:12:35,340 --> 00:12:39,240
simulating a smallpox attack on America.

246
00:12:39,340 --> 00:12:41,560
Already, communications are breaking down

247
00:12:41,660 --> 00:12:44,120
among the exercise participants.

248
00:12:44,220 --> 00:12:46,060
Police, medical, and government personnel

249
00:12:46,061 --> 00:12:48,400
are having trouble figuring out chain of command.

250
00:12:48,500 --> 00:12:49,900
Slassen and Telegraph.

251
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,560
Citizens in the affected states are starting to panic.

252
00:12:55,400 --> 00:12:58,140
The threat of a large-scale chemical or biological attack,

253
00:12:58,240 --> 00:13:00,900
like the one in the dark winter scenario,

254
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:04,720
has led some Americans to purchase gas masks.

255
00:13:04,820 --> 00:13:08,420
Most consumer gas masks work by filtering contaminated air

256
00:13:08,520 --> 00:13:11,260
through a special canister.

257
00:13:11,360 --> 00:13:13,160
Air first passes through a HEPA filter,

258
00:13:13,260 --> 00:13:15,020
which removes the tiny particles that

259
00:13:15,021 --> 00:13:17,620
that may contain viruses, bacteria,

260
00:13:17,621 --> 00:13:20,320
and other biological agents.

261
00:13:20,321 --> 00:13:22,560
Next, a specially treated bed of carbon

262
00:13:22,561 --> 00:13:26,360
filters out gases like sarin, VX, and mustard gas.

263
00:13:30,540 --> 00:13:34,740
Although they were widely used by soldiers in World War I,

264
00:13:34,741 --> 00:13:37,680
the first wide-scale use of gas masks by civilians

265
00:13:37,681 --> 00:13:42,880
took place in Britain during the German Blitz of World War II.

266
00:13:42,881 --> 00:13:44,520
The British government was so certain

267
00:13:44,521 --> 00:13:47,920
that the Nazis would use gas warfare against their cities,

268
00:13:47,921 --> 00:13:52,660
that masks were issued to all citizens.

269
00:13:52,661 --> 00:13:56,000
Most Britons got the general civilian respirator,

270
00:13:56,001 --> 00:13:58,100
which was molded from a single piece of rubber

271
00:13:58,101 --> 00:13:59,560
and came in four different sizes.

272
00:14:02,300 --> 00:14:04,300
The helmet respirator was designed for people

273
00:14:04,301 --> 00:14:07,340
with special medical needs, like asthma.

274
00:14:07,341 --> 00:14:12,440
It used a hand-powered bellows to assist with breathing.

275
00:14:12,441 --> 00:14:15,180
The baby protective helmet worked much the same way.

276
00:14:17,980 --> 00:14:20,340
Over 100 million civilian respirators

277
00:14:20,440 --> 00:14:22,480
were distributed in Britain during the war.

278
00:14:25,520 --> 00:14:27,980
Despite months of conventional bombing,

279
00:14:28,080 --> 00:14:31,900
the German gas attack never came.

280
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,820
During World War II, there was no wide-scale distribution

281
00:14:34,920 --> 00:14:38,600
of gas masks to American civilians.

282
00:14:38,700 --> 00:14:41,240
But immediately after the bombing of Pearl Harbor,

283
00:14:41,241 --> 00:14:46,080
Noncombatant masks were issued to civilians in Hawaii.

284
00:14:46,180 --> 00:14:48,180
Every child got one.

285
00:14:48,280 --> 00:14:51,280
Some were even issued the so-called bunny mask,

286
00:14:51,380 --> 00:14:54,580
which was meant to make gas mask wearing fun for kids.

287
00:14:57,520 --> 00:15:00,260
The first wide-scale civilian distribution of gas masks

288
00:15:00,360 --> 00:15:04,000
since World War II took place in Israel in 1990

289
00:15:04,100 --> 00:15:07,300
at the start of the first Gulf War.

290
00:15:07,400 --> 00:15:10,160
With Iraqi Scud missiles targeting their country,

291
00:15:10,161 --> 00:15:14,260
Israeli officials feared a chemical attack.

292
00:15:14,360 --> 00:15:17,100
Just as the British had done 50 years earlier,

293
00:15:17,200 --> 00:15:22,140
the Israeli government issued gas masks to all citizens.

294
00:15:22,240 --> 00:15:27,040
As in Britain, the chemical attack never came.

295
00:15:27,140 --> 00:15:30,240
According to the Associated Press, two Israelis

296
00:15:30,340 --> 00:15:34,120
died during the war from conventional scud attacks.

297
00:15:34,220 --> 00:15:37,960
But 11 died in the panic, either from heart attacks

298
00:15:37,961 --> 00:15:41,660
or misuse of gas masks.

299
00:15:41,760 --> 00:15:43,860
Still, many Americans are wondering

300
00:15:43,960 --> 00:15:46,800
if they should buy masks to protect themselves from attack

301
00:15:46,900 --> 00:15:50,000
with unconventional weapons.

302
00:15:50,100 --> 00:15:52,200
In the days after September 11, there

303
00:15:52,300 --> 00:15:57,720
was a run on gas masks at military surplus stores.

304
00:15:57,820 --> 00:16:00,920
Not all masks are created equal, so it's important to know

305
00:16:01,020 --> 00:16:03,020
what you're buying.

306
00:16:03,120 --> 00:16:04,420
To don this mask...

307
00:16:04,520 --> 00:16:06,920
Dan Skrimsher is an expert on protection

308
00:16:06,921 --> 00:16:09,380
from chemical and biological terrorism.

309
00:16:09,480 --> 00:16:11,720
His company, Tricon Environmental,

310
00:16:11,820 --> 00:16:14,760
sells a wide range of gas masks, including

311
00:16:14,860 --> 00:16:19,360
the affordable Survive Air Quick 2000 Escape Hood.

312
00:16:19,460 --> 00:16:23,300
It comes in a heavy-duty seal foil bag.

313
00:16:23,400 --> 00:16:26,700
Nothing more than tearing the top of the bag off.

314
00:16:26,800 --> 00:16:31,580
And just pull the mask out, open it up, and insert your head.

315
00:16:31,680 --> 00:16:34,780
It has a nose clip inside that you can pull to adjust

316
00:16:34,880 --> 00:16:36,380
to fit your nose.

317
00:16:36,381 --> 00:16:41,280
This mask can sell for $126 to $176.

318
00:16:41,380 --> 00:16:44,220
This mask has upwards to a two-hour rating,

319
00:16:44,320 --> 00:16:48,360
no more than that, for chemical, biological, smoke, dust,

320
00:16:48,460 --> 00:16:51,520
fumes, mist.

321
00:16:51,620 --> 00:16:54,160
The neoteric homeland hood promises

322
00:16:54,260 --> 00:16:57,960
the added protection of nuclear, biological, and chemical,

323
00:16:58,060 --> 00:17:01,480
or NBC, filters.

324
00:17:01,580 --> 00:17:04,300
It will filter out certain chemical agents,

325
00:17:04,301 --> 00:17:10,840
as well as two forms of nuclear contamination, alpha and beta.

326
00:17:10,940 --> 00:17:14,540
It will not filter out gamma.

327
00:17:14,640 --> 00:17:16,880
The OptiFit full facepiece respirator

328
00:17:16,980 --> 00:17:19,920
also uses NBC filters.

329
00:17:20,020 --> 00:17:22,660
In field tests, it protected the wearer from mustard gas

330
00:17:22,760 --> 00:17:24,660
for over two hours.

331
00:17:24,760 --> 00:17:28,620
It costs around $200.

332
00:17:28,720 --> 00:17:32,500
Even the best mask is worthless without a good seal.

333
00:17:32,501 --> 00:17:36,000
A good way to tell if you have a seal

334
00:17:36,100 --> 00:17:39,200
is to simply close off for just a moment

335
00:17:39,300 --> 00:17:42,240
the inlet to the cartridges, breathe in or exhale

336
00:17:42,340 --> 00:17:45,440
to see if there's a negative pressure felt inside the mask.

337
00:17:45,540 --> 00:17:49,660
If there is, you should have a good seal on the face.

338
00:17:49,760 --> 00:17:51,160
For even more protection,

339
00:17:51,260 --> 00:17:54,360
you can get a full chemical biological protective suit.

340
00:17:57,300 --> 00:18:00,460
These are primarily sold to the law enforcement, EMS,

341
00:18:00,461 --> 00:18:02,660
and medical and fire department personnel.

342
00:18:02,760 --> 00:18:06,600
However, we have sold some of these to private citizens

343
00:18:06,700 --> 00:18:09,060
for their own personal use.

344
00:18:09,160 --> 00:18:13,400
This may be a bit much for home protection.

345
00:18:13,500 --> 00:18:16,500
This strange-looking battery-powered contraption

346
00:18:16,600 --> 00:18:21,220
promises to protect your baby from contaminated air.

347
00:18:21,320 --> 00:18:24,160
It even comes with a pouch that can deliver formula

348
00:18:24,260 --> 00:18:27,020
to your infant through a tube.

349
00:18:27,021 --> 00:18:32,700
You have the option of carrying your infant out of harm's way,

350
00:18:32,960 --> 00:18:36,960
or you can also transport the infant in a backpack mode

351
00:18:36,961 --> 00:18:39,920
by simply taking the straps and applying it to your back.

352
00:18:41,280 --> 00:18:44,740
There is also a respirator available for slightly older children.

353
00:18:45,800 --> 00:18:49,000
It looks a lot like its counterpart from half a century ago,

354
00:18:49,420 --> 00:18:53,340
only this model uses a motorized blower to get air into the suit.

355
00:18:57,020 --> 00:19:00,320
And thanks to technology, no member of the family

356
00:19:00,420 --> 00:19:02,420
needs to go unprotected.

357
00:19:02,520 --> 00:19:05,320
Energizing the units.

358
00:19:05,420 --> 00:19:08,760
The pet shield promises to give your dog or cat

359
00:19:08,860 --> 00:19:12,560
six hours' worth of contaminant-free air.

360
00:19:12,660 --> 00:19:15,580
Some experts doubt that masks and respirators

361
00:19:15,680 --> 00:19:19,080
are necessary for the average person.

362
00:19:19,180 --> 00:19:21,480
What the government recommends is being prepared

363
00:19:21,580 --> 00:19:26,420
to seal up doors and vents in a room in your house.

364
00:19:26,421 --> 00:19:29,560
Duct tape is commonly recommended,

365
00:19:29,660 --> 00:19:34,220
but a new product called Chem Tape promises to provide extra

366
00:19:34,320 --> 00:19:37,060
protection against chemical attack.

367
00:19:37,160 --> 00:19:40,060
It's important to have a seal all the way around

368
00:19:40,160 --> 00:19:43,940
to keep out any inward filtration of any outside contaminants

369
00:19:44,040 --> 00:19:46,080
into your home.

370
00:19:46,180 --> 00:19:49,880
It's recommended that you pre-cut large sheets of plastic

371
00:19:49,980 --> 00:19:53,220
to fit the doors in your safe room.

372
00:19:53,320 --> 00:19:55,380
Once we reach the bottom of the door,

373
00:19:55,381 --> 00:19:58,680
of the door, we want to make sure we get a seal on the floor

374
00:19:58,780 --> 00:20:01,980
to prevent any inward leakage of contaminants

375
00:20:02,080 --> 00:20:06,720
under the door seal or door frame.

376
00:20:06,820 --> 00:20:09,220
Sealing yourself in a room might protect you

377
00:20:09,320 --> 00:20:11,120
from an attack at home.

378
00:20:11,220 --> 00:20:15,640
But what can technology do to safeguard you at the stadium?

379
00:20:15,740 --> 00:20:18,640
Next, see what's being done to take you safely

380
00:20:18,740 --> 00:20:20,040
out to the ball game.

381
00:20:25,380 --> 00:20:29,380
It's now several days into the dark winter war game,

382
00:20:29,480 --> 00:20:32,880
simulating a bioterror attack on America.

383
00:20:32,980 --> 00:20:37,320
And already, interstate commerce has collapsed.

384
00:20:37,420 --> 00:20:39,920
The National Security Council is mulling the need

385
00:20:40,020 --> 00:20:43,040
to declare martial law.

386
00:20:43,140 --> 00:20:46,000
It's becoming clear that America is not prepared

387
00:20:46,100 --> 00:20:49,440
for this new type of threat.

388
00:20:49,540 --> 00:20:53,500
Recent history provides many examples of terrorist attacks

389
00:20:53,501 --> 00:20:59,080
attacks on public places, cafes in Israel, Olympic Park

390
00:20:59,180 --> 00:21:00,580
in Atlanta.

391
00:21:00,680 --> 00:21:06,240
But these attacks have been with conventional weapons, bombs.

392
00:21:06,340 --> 00:21:10,320
In 1995, though, the Aum Shinrikyo cult in Japan

393
00:21:10,420 --> 00:21:13,280
unleashed a chemical attack on the Tokyo subway system

394
00:21:13,380 --> 00:21:16,220
using sarin nerve gas.

395
00:21:16,320 --> 00:21:18,660
Hundreds were hospitalized.

396
00:21:18,760 --> 00:21:20,920
12 died.

397
00:21:21,020 --> 00:21:22,520
A lot of people also don't realize, though,

398
00:21:22,521 --> 00:21:24,480
is in addition to the use of sarin,

399
00:21:24,580 --> 00:21:28,060
the Amshinrikyo cult also attempted on a number of occasions

400
00:21:28,160 --> 00:21:32,360
to disseminate biological weapons in various cities in Japan.

401
00:21:32,460 --> 00:21:34,560
As far as we know, nobody became ill.

402
00:21:34,660 --> 00:21:37,460
Nobody died from those events.

403
00:21:37,560 --> 00:21:39,280
There are even ominous reports

404
00:21:39,380 --> 00:21:41,180
that cult members traveled to Africa

405
00:21:41,280 --> 00:21:44,780
during an outbreak of the deadly hemorrhagic fever Ebola.

406
00:21:44,880 --> 00:21:46,920
The concern is that they might have been trying to isolate

407
00:21:47,020 --> 00:21:51,820
the Ebola virus for potential use as a terrorist weapon.

408
00:21:51,821 --> 00:21:55,260
Could a terrorist group attack subway systems in American cities

409
00:21:55,360 --> 00:21:57,960
with chemical or biological agents?

410
00:21:58,060 --> 00:22:00,800
What about other public places?

411
00:22:00,900 --> 00:22:03,100
I think that gatherings of a large number of people

412
00:22:03,200 --> 00:22:06,160
in public places is, in fact, one of the areas where we have

413
00:22:06,260 --> 00:22:08,440
to be vigilant about our security.

414
00:22:08,540 --> 00:22:11,440
Congress was worried enough about chemical, biological,

415
00:22:11,540 --> 00:22:13,980
and radiological threats to public places

416
00:22:14,080 --> 00:22:17,640
that in 1999, they authorized the creation of special National

417
00:22:17,641 --> 00:22:23,640
called Weapons of Mass Destruction Civil Support Teams.

418
00:22:23,740 --> 00:22:25,340
Stationed throughout the country,

419
00:22:25,440 --> 00:22:29,180
these teams conduct security pre-sweeps of major events

420
00:22:29,280 --> 00:22:32,260
and are also trained to assist emergency first responders

421
00:22:32,360 --> 00:22:35,320
in the case of an actual attack.

422
00:22:35,420 --> 00:22:39,160
If there's ever a possibility that any sort of terror

423
00:22:39,260 --> 00:22:42,060
or weapons of mass destruction incident may take place,

424
00:22:42,160 --> 00:22:44,960
that's where the civil support teams here in the nation

425
00:22:45,060 --> 00:22:47,640
may become involved.

426
00:22:47,641 --> 00:22:53,880
In 2003, a drill was held at an arena in Northern California

427
00:22:53,980 --> 00:22:57,180
using a scenario in which terrorists released cyanide

428
00:22:57,280 --> 00:22:58,580
during a basketball game.

429
00:23:03,480 --> 00:23:07,300
While law enforcement searches the arena for perpetrators

430
00:23:07,400 --> 00:23:12,840
and medics tend to the sick, the National Guard civil support

431
00:23:12,940 --> 00:23:16,600
team rolls into action.

432
00:23:16,601 --> 00:23:19,700
They wear full level A protective gear as they sweep

433
00:23:19,800 --> 00:23:23,240
the arena with their chemical and biological detection

434
00:23:23,340 --> 00:23:25,080
devices.

435
00:23:25,180 --> 00:23:27,880
They are able to quickly locate the source of the cyanide

436
00:23:27,980 --> 00:23:29,740
as the local authorities successfully

437
00:23:29,840 --> 00:23:33,820
apprehend the mock terrorist.

438
00:23:33,920 --> 00:23:36,280
The first responders returning from the scene

439
00:23:36,380 --> 00:23:38,960
undergo a careful decontamination process

440
00:23:39,060 --> 00:23:41,020
under the supervision of the National Guard.

441
00:23:41,120 --> 00:23:42,620
I'm going to go ahead and check you

442
00:23:42,720 --> 00:23:44,960
and make sure that all the decontamination

443
00:23:44,961 --> 00:23:47,400
The contamination is off your body.

444
00:23:47,401 --> 00:23:48,460
This was only a drill.

445
00:23:48,461 --> 00:23:49,800
Throw your stuff in the bag here.

446
00:23:49,801 --> 00:23:52,940
But the reality of the threat has brought California's ninth

447
00:23:52,941 --> 00:23:55,880
weapons of mass destruction civil support team

448
00:23:55,881 --> 00:23:59,300
to the World Series, the Super Bowl, and on this day,

449
00:23:59,301 --> 00:24:01,400
to the Stanley Cup hockey championships

450
00:24:01,401 --> 00:24:04,320
at the Arrowhead Pond in Anaheim, California,

451
00:24:04,321 --> 00:24:08,160
where they've been asked to conduct a pre-sweep of the venue.

452
00:24:08,161 --> 00:24:11,920
10 AM, four hours before the first spectators

453
00:24:11,921 --> 00:24:16,920
trickling into the arena for the sold-out game.

454
00:24:17,020 --> 00:24:19,020
Six hours until the puck will drop

455
00:24:19,120 --> 00:24:21,120
before 20,000 screaming fans.

456
00:24:25,460 --> 00:24:28,460
At a fire department training facility near the arena,

457
00:24:28,560 --> 00:24:32,380
the National Guard team has established a staging area.

458
00:24:32,480 --> 00:24:34,580
As soon as they arrive, the survey team

459
00:24:34,680 --> 00:24:36,320
prepares for the pre-sweep.

460
00:24:36,420 --> 00:24:38,720
They will be using the latest in high-tech gear

461
00:24:38,721 --> 00:24:40,920
to search for any chemical, biological,

462
00:24:41,020 --> 00:24:43,060
or radiological materials.

463
00:24:43,160 --> 00:24:45,160
In this particular case, what we're able to do

464
00:24:45,260 --> 00:24:48,360
is actually check on the stadium and the surrounding areas

465
00:24:48,460 --> 00:24:51,260
and actually check in advance and make certain assessments

466
00:24:51,360 --> 00:24:53,400
based on what might be potential threats

467
00:24:53,500 --> 00:24:55,640
in order to safeguard the people before the event even

468
00:24:55,740 --> 00:24:57,840
takes place.

469
00:24:57,940 --> 00:25:01,080
11 AM, the survey team members gather up their gear

470
00:25:01,180 --> 00:25:02,680
and depart for the Anaheim Park.

471
00:25:08,720 --> 00:25:13,720
Staff Sergeant Garudin's first task is to gather air samples

472
00:25:13,820 --> 00:25:17,720
from several locations around the arena using a device

473
00:25:17,820 --> 00:25:20,460
called the BT-550.

474
00:25:20,560 --> 00:25:23,660
We just sit it down in the room, let it run.

475
00:25:23,760 --> 00:25:26,580
And when it's running, the working light will be on.

476
00:25:26,680 --> 00:25:28,980
And then when it stops, because you can hear it, it'll stop.

477
00:25:29,080 --> 00:25:30,580
Then we know that it's complete.

478
00:25:30,581 --> 00:25:32,380
And then when it stops, because you can hear it,

479
00:25:32,381 --> 00:25:33,920
it'll stop, then we know that it's complete.

480
00:25:38,480 --> 00:25:42,200
The team also has equipment for detecting chemical agents.

481
00:25:42,201 --> 00:25:45,540
This is our AKADA Army Chemical Agent Detector.

482
00:25:45,541 --> 00:25:47,480
Basically, it's an area monitor.

483
00:25:47,481 --> 00:25:52,940
It monitors for chemical nerve and blister type agents.

484
00:25:52,941 --> 00:25:55,540
If the AKADA's alarm goes off, the survey team

485
00:25:55,541 --> 00:25:59,200
uses another device, a handheld detector called the ICAN

486
00:25:59,201 --> 00:26:02,700
to find the source of the trouble.

487
00:26:02,701 --> 00:26:05,140
Like an arcana takes a whole room perspective.

488
00:26:05,141 --> 00:26:06,740
It lets you know that there's something there,

489
00:26:06,741 --> 00:26:08,600
and the eye cam pinpoints.

490
00:26:08,601 --> 00:26:11,280
You know there's an agent there, a suspected agent.

491
00:26:11,281 --> 00:26:12,880
They just take the eye cam and it can pinpoint

492
00:26:12,881 --> 00:26:16,160
like it's in this area and it's not, you know, over there.

493
00:26:16,161 --> 00:26:19,020
Then we can clear that area.

494
00:26:19,021 --> 00:26:22,420
While the survey team is busy at the arena,

495
00:26:22,421 --> 00:26:24,920
the science officers prepare the mobile lab

496
00:26:24,921 --> 00:26:27,460
to receive their samples.

497
00:26:27,461 --> 00:26:31,400
at 1 o'clock p.m. A call comes in from the survey team.

498
00:26:31,500 --> 00:26:34,000
Their chemical detectors are getting a positive reading

499
00:26:34,100 --> 00:26:38,400
on a fluid in a supply closet at the arena.

500
00:26:38,500 --> 00:26:40,100
The team suspects that the substance

501
00:26:40,200 --> 00:26:42,500
is a harmless cleaning solution,

502
00:26:42,600 --> 00:26:45,220
but they're not taking any chances.

503
00:26:45,320 --> 00:26:46,920
A sample of it will be brought back,

504
00:26:47,020 --> 00:26:51,120
along with the air samples, for further analysis.

505
00:26:51,220 --> 00:26:52,920
We're just bringing it back just to see and make sure

506
00:26:53,020 --> 00:26:54,620
before people get into the stadium

507
00:26:54,621 --> 00:26:58,220
But it's definitely a clean solution.

508
00:26:58,320 --> 00:26:59,920
If any of the samples they bring back

509
00:27:00,020 --> 00:27:02,920
are confirmed as positive for chemical, biological,

510
00:27:03,020 --> 00:27:05,500
or radiological materials, the authorities

511
00:27:05,600 --> 00:27:07,760
will want to know what areas around the stadium

512
00:27:07,860 --> 00:27:09,060
need to be evacuated.

513
00:27:12,500 --> 00:27:14,380
Sergeant First Class Robert Goldsmith

514
00:27:14,480 --> 00:27:19,380
creates computer models of how various agents might spread.

515
00:27:19,480 --> 00:27:24,380
He uses current weather information to model the plume.

516
00:27:24,381 --> 00:27:28,880
You put in a type of suspected device, suspected agent,

517
00:27:28,881 --> 00:27:31,780
based off of what they get hits on downrange.

518
00:27:31,781 --> 00:27:35,260
You plug in information, humidity, temperature, wind

519
00:27:35,261 --> 00:27:36,560
speed, wind direction.

520
00:27:36,561 --> 00:27:37,720
It does the work itself.

521
00:27:40,500 --> 00:27:43,600
1.15 PM, the survey team returns from the arena

522
00:27:43,601 --> 00:27:44,440
with their samples.

523
00:27:47,500 --> 00:27:50,340
The first step is to bring them into the airtight glove box

